>Split gehennom into three branches, lawful, chaotic, and neutral,

Slash'EM has gotten me a bit tired of alignment-themed branches for
now. Also, a branching Hell exists in Linley's Dungeon Crawl, but I'm
not able to gauge how effective that was.

Here's my idea for improving Gehennom that I sporked from a number of
posts some years back that I don't feel like linking to: a number of
randomly chosen special levels that contain explicit game rule
exceptions.

For example:
* Floor of Resurrection - every corpse on this level will revive like
trolls do. The "summon nasties" spell suddenly becomes extra potent -
as do pets. Cockatrice corpses, meanwhile, are less potent.
* Floor of Gravity - on this floor, every object weights double it
normally does. Not only must you figure out how to lug your bag across
the level, but blunt objects (especially boulders) will do more damage.
* Floor of Pw Drain - your Pw is reduced by one each turn. This will
certainly influence more classes than others, but there you go. (Maybe
spellcasting monsters should be hindered as well?)
* Lode Runner Floor - On this floor, demolished walls "grow back" and
pits in the floor fill themselves! What's more, any poor creature
inside the pit while it fills is buried alive! (Easier to implement
when the DevTeam - or someone - finishes the "buried monster" code -
the framework of which is in dig.c)
* Unmappable Floor - well, I'll link this one:
(http://groups.google.com/group/rec.games.roguelike.nethack/browse_thread/thread/8467d56681cdc3/fe3923b487ade461)

Perhaps some of these floors could double as demon prince floors.



